e suspect alive  once it was clear that he would live the  cia took custody of him in america this  was announced as huge news among those  we have captured is a man named abu  zabeda  al-qaeda's chief of operations  from him  and from hundreds of others  we are learning more about how the  terrorists plan and operate  our coalition is strong  but back in pakistan the people who are  detaining him discovered something  disturbing  abu zubaydah was not exactly the guy  they thought he was you see while he was  fighting in afghanistan abu zubair was  hit in the head by some shrapnel some of  that shrapnel lodged in his brain and  gave him serious brain damage he  developed severe memory problems and  couldn't speak for a year so the man  they had arrested was not a senior al  qaeda operative he wasn't a senior  operative in anything he was a greeter  in a training camp in afghanistan but it  was not an al qaeda camp it was opposed  to al qaeda the cia had even helped set  it up in the 80s  when the government found abu zubair's  diary thousands of pages long recording  my new details of people he met or what  he ate every day they found nothing  relating to the terrorist attacks in  which he was allegedly involved  what they found instead was the story of  a mentally ill individual whose head  injury resulted in his writing the diary  in any one of three different  personalities the fbi senior expert on  al qaeda said himself this guy is insane  certifiable split personality  the diaries showed that abu zubaydah  simply wasn't involved in al qaeda he  wrote more about his deep attachment to  his mother than about any involvement in  terrorist activities  here's one diary entry  now i don't cry when i remember my dad  beating up my mom  i don't cry when i remember her crying  like a child in my arms when i was a  child  i don't cry when i remember all these  things as much as i cry when i remember  that i offended her  this was not exactly the master  terrorist the u.s thought they had  captured but the u.s government had  already said very publicly that this guy  was a top operative in al qaeda and a  trusted confidant of osama bin laden  among those we have captured is a man  named abu zabeda  al-qaeda's chief of operations  so in april 2002 president bush turned  to his cia director and told him  i said he was important you're not going  to let me lose face on this are you the  cia director responded that no he  wouldn't let the president lose face so  what was initially a mistake was  declared to be true and became a  fabrication  abu zubaydah became the first high-value  detainee of the war on terror two weeks  after his capture he was transported to  a black site in thailand where he could  be subject to a new method of  interrogation the cia was testing out  almost immediately after abu zubaida was  captured the cia began discussing how to  interpret the us's prohibition on  torture in order to allow for what they  called enhanced interrogation  and at the black site  they put that plan into effect here's  how a government report described the  atmosphere of the prison  the cable described abu zubaida's cell  as white with no natural lighting or  windows but with four halogen lights  pointed into the cell  security officers wore all black  uniforms including boots gloves  balaclavas and goggles to keep abu  zubaydah from identifying the officers  as well as to prevent abu subaida from  seeing the security guards as  individuals who he may attempt to  establish a relationship or dialogue  with  the security officers communicated by  hand signals when they were with abu's  beta and used handcuffs and leg shackles  to maintain control  in addition either loud rock music was  played or noise generators were used to  enhance abu zubaydah's sense of  hopelessness  abu zubaido was typically kept naked and  sleep deprived  during his time in thailand abu zubaydah  was kept in terrible conditions at the  beginning he was given no solid food and  kept naked and shackled to a chair  after a few weeks he was given rice once  a day and allowed to sleep naked on the  floor  abu sabha was kept totally isolated for  an entire month while the cia developed  a plan for more aggressive torture  in late july they began testing a  procedure called waterboarding in which  a prisoner would be strapped to a table  with water poured over their head in  order to simulate the experience of  drowning in august 2002 alone abu  zubaydah was waterboarded 83 times  for 17 straight days the cia used a  variety of techniques waterboarding  slamming him against a concrete wall  confining him in a small box depriving  him of sleep blasting white noise and  keeping him in a freezing room as the  torture continued abu zubair became so  broken and compliant that when an  interrogator snapped his finger twice  abuzabado would lie down flat to be  waterboarded in at least one session he  became completely unresponsive totally  desensitized to the torture  all of this was filmed by the cia and 92  tapes were produced to what was done in  thailand but while they were torturing  abu zubaydah the cia began to find that  he wasn't really able to provide them  much information  the cia still thought he was holding  back so they continued torturing him  in order to prevent further torture abu  zubaydah told them what they wanted to  hear  he told them that al qaeda wanted to  attack the brooklyn bridge and the  statue of liberty and that they knew how  to build a nuclear weapon and smuggle it  into america he even told them that  al-qaeda had ties to saddam hussein's  government in iraq  all of these false statements were used  by the bush administration to justify  expanding the war on terror  obliterated statements were a big part  of the intelligence that america cited  in invading iraq the next year  but what abu zubaida was telling his  torturers was never actually useful  according to the washington post not a  single significant plot was foiled as a  result of abu zubaida's tortured  confessions  soon the cia began to have doubts about  the information of zubaida was providing  and about their methods in getting that  information some of them even requested  that if abuzabeta died during  interrogation his body be cremated so  that the torture wouldn't be discovered  so in 2005 a cia agent named gina haspel  the person who oversaw the black site in  thailand ordered the destruction of 90  videotapes of abu zubaida's torture  in september 2006 abu zubaydah left cia  custody and was transferred to  guantanamo bay america's main detention  camp for enemy combatants by then the  cia had already concluded that abu  zubaydah was in fact  not even a member of al-qaeda  by the time that abuza arrived in  guantanamo the torture had fully broken  him and that was more than a decade ago  to this day abu zubaydah is still in  captivity in guantanamo the country that  tortured him having simply forgotten  about him it never mattered that they  knew he was innocent just look at what  happened to the people who tortured him  was the beta gina haspel who oversaw was  the beta's torture became director of  the cia in 2018. thank you again for  giving me the opportunity to serve and  to carry out the critical work of  helping protect our country our people  and our way of life thank you very much  president bush retired to his ranch in  texas please welcome president george w  bush  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  meanwhile is still in guantanamo bay he  has never been charged with anything  more than 10 years after the government  concluded he wasn't a terrorist he's  still held in strict detention  my mother he said in one letter to the  outside world i have not seen her in so  long  i'm a smile good feet for the gravel  institute  [Music]  you

